    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required **all** rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`. The rejections come from ~10 separate `@ControllerAdvice` request-body advices, each with its own `@ExceptionHandler` returning a plain-string or `Map` body (`text/plain` / `application/json`).\n\nRather than edit every one of those handlers (which the cost model penalizes heavily \u2014 each change summed and then multiplied by the number of files touched), I added a **single new servlet filter**, `ProblemJsonRejectionFilter`:\n\n- Wraps the response with `ContentCachingResponseWrapper`, then after the chain runs, checks the final status.\n- If it's 400/409/429 **and** the body isn't already `application/problem+json`, it rewrites the body into an RFC7807 document (`type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`) with the correct content type; the HTTP status is preserved.\n- Responses that a handler already rendered as problem+json (e.g. the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice` output) are passed through untouched, so nothing is double-processed.\n\n**Structural impact:** one new file, zero existing files touched, and no existing method or class grows \u2014 the four small methods in the new class each carry low complexity. This satisfies the rule globally regardless of which advice produced the rejection.",
